window.docContents[237].resolve({"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.ISA.Memory/#file-Arm64___Trusted___ISA___Memory":{"contents":"Paper › arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/ISA › Memory.leanTier 3 · arm64-semantics\n\nTier 3 · arm64-semantics used by the symbolic proof 4 headline theorems depend on it 267 of 397 lines trusted source ↗Trusted, reviewed with the engine. The AArch64 model in arm64-semantics is Tier 3: it is reviewed in its own repository (check-trust-guards). The declarations that the headline statements use are shown for completeness.\n\nDeclarations on this page (45)structure Permdef protNonedef rwdef rxdef rwxdef frozendef _root_.Option.mappeddef _root_.Option.rdef _root_.Option.wdef _root_.Option.xdef _root_.Option.frozenstructure Memorydef rawPermdef permdef isLazyFreedef isAnondef read8def write8def read16def write16def read32def write32def read64def write64def readSizeddef writeSizeddef read128def write128def readSimdSizeddef writeSimdSizeddef readBytesdef writeBytesdef allPermdef allNoLazyFreedef allAnondef canReaddef canWritedef canExecdef canMapdef countPermdef countRwdef Frozendef KeepsFrozendef mapZerodef SimdMemSize.bytes\n\nARM64 Byte-Addressable Little-Endian Virtual Memory (Arm64.Memory)* Layer / Role: Arm64 Trusted Computing Base (Role::Spec, definitions only, 0 theorems).* Purpose & Key Exports: 64-bit byte-addressable virtual memory (Memory) mapping addresses to per-byte optional permissions and bytes (cells : BitVec 64 → Option (Option Perm × BitVec 8)), protection flags (Perm, Perm.protNone, Perm.ro, Perm.rw, Perm.rx, canRead, canWrite, canExec, canMap, countPerm, countRw), little-endian accessors (read8, read16, read32, read64, read128, write8, write16, write32, write64, write128, readBytes, writeBytes), and mapping helpers (mapFresh, mapZero, unmapWritable, unmapNonFrozen, setPermNonFrozen, zeroNonFrozen).* When to read: Use this header or lean-sig outline Arm64/Trusted/ISA/Memory.lean for API names; use ProofLib/Memory.lean and Arm64/Trusted/Spec/SepLogic.lean for reasoning about memory in proofs.Conservative Permissions, Option Perm, & W^Xread*/write* ignore permissions; Arm64.execInst checks canRead/canWrite/canExec before every access and faults otherwise. Writes never alter permissions. A byte's permission in Memory is m.perm a : Option Perm:* none is unmapped (no VMA). Permissions are conservative (at most the real machine's): \"unmapped\" (none) means \"not known to be accessible\", not \"guaranteed to fault\", so proofs must avoid unmapped bytes without deriving facts from unmappedness.* some p is mapped ((m.perm a).mapped = (m.perm a).isSome = true) with Linux protection bits p : Perm (r, w, x). In particular, some Perm.protNone (some ⟨false, false, false⟩) is a mapped PROT_NONE page: reserved in the virtual address space (canMap = false, so mmap and brk never overlap it), inaccessible (canRead = canWrite = canExec = false), and not frozen (frozen = false).* Executable memory is never writable (canWrite requires w ∧ ¬x, W^X), ensuring loaded instructions remain immutable across execution.\n\n\n\nstructure PermL47–58 ↗#Linux protection bits (PROT_READ, PROT_WRITE, PROT_EXEC) and per-byte VM state of a mapped byte of virtual memory: readable (r), writable (w), executable (x), whether the byte is marked lazyfree via madvise(..., MADV_FREE) (lazyFree, asynchronously zeroable until dirtied by a store), and whether the byte belongs to an anonymous mapping (anon, false on file-backed PT_LOAD pages).\n\n\n\ndef protNoneL62–64 ↗#Mapped with PROT_NONE (---): reserved in the virtual address space, inaccessible to loads, stores, and instruction fetches (r = w = x = false), and not frozen (frozen = false).\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 65–66): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Perm.ro\n\ndef rwL67–68 ↗#Read-write (rw-).\n\ndef rxL69–70 ↗#Read-execute (r-x), for code.\n\ndef rwxL72–75 ↗#Normalized 3-bit protection projection (r, w, x) with default lazyFree = false and anon = true.\n\ndef frozenL77–79 ↗#The mapped byte is read-only or executable and not writable. Its contents and permission are frozen: nothing during execution may change them (see Memory.KeepsFrozen).\n\n\n\ndef _root_.Option.mappedL83–84 ↗#An optional byte permission is mapped iff it is some p (isSome).\n\ndef _root_.Option.rL86–90 ↗#An optional byte permission is readable iff it is some q with q.r = true.\n\ndef _root_.Option.wL92–96 ↗#An optional byte permission is writable iff it is some q with q.w = true.\n\ndef _root_.Option.xL98–102 ↗#An optional byte permission is executable iff it is some q with q.x = true.\n\ndef _root_.Option.frozenL104–108 ↗#An optional byte permission is frozen iff it is some q with q.frozen = true.\n\nstructure MemoryL110–131 ↗#Byte-addressable 64-bit virtual memory: a partial map from addresses to an optional permission (Option Perm) and a byte (BitVec 8). none is an untouched address (unmapped, byte 0).Representation. Lean core and Std provide no proof-friendly partial map over the full 64-bit address space: Std.TreeMap / Std.HashMap are finite, computational structures whose reasoning principles are about insertion histories, not pointwise lookups. A total function into Option is the simplest faithful model and makes pointwise lemmas (read-over-write, permission-over-write) one-line case splits.Interface. Clients use the derived accessors read8 (the byte, 0 if absent) and perm (Option Perm: none if unmapped, some p if mapped with protection p) together with the base lemmas of ProofLib.Invariant / ProofLib.CoreLemmas (read8_write8, perm_write8, perm_mapFresh, ...), never cells directly.Writes to unmapped addresses. write8 stores some (m.perm a, v), so a raw write to an unmapped address (m.perm a = none) creates some (none, v) with permission none (unmapped), never some (some ⟨false, false, false⟩, v). This keeps read8_write8 and perm_write8 unconditional while reserving some ⟨false, false, false⟩ (some Perm.protNone) exclusively for mapped PROT_NONE pages.\n\n\n\n2 declarations not shown (lines 135–144): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Memory.empty, Arm64.Memory.ofFn\n\ndef rawPermL146–150 ↗#The raw optional cell permission at addr (including lazyFree and anon bits).\n\ndef permL152–157 ↗#The normalized 3-bit protection (r, w, x) of the byte at addr (none if unmapped, some p.rwx if mapped with p). Invariant under stores (write8) and madvise.\n\ndef isLazyFreeL159–164 ↗#Whether the byte at addr is mapped and marked lazyfree via MADV_FREE (so its contents may be asynchronously reclaimed to zero by Linux 7.3 until overwritten).\n\ndef isAnonL166–171 ↗#Whether the byte at addr is mapped as anonymous memory (vma_is_anonymous on Linux 7.3; false on unmapped bytes and file-backed PT_LOAD pages).\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 173–178): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Memory.permNoLazy\n\ndef read8L180–184 ↗#Read a single byte (8 bits) at addr (0 if unmapped).\n\ndef write8L186–197 ↗#Write a single byte (8 bits) at addr. Normalized permissions (m.perm) and m.isAnon are unchanged; writing clears lazyFree := false (storing dirties the PTE on Linux 7.3, cancelling lazy reclaim).\n\ndef read16L199–203 ↗#Read a 16-bit halfword in little-endian byte order at addr.\n\ndef write16L205–209 ↗#Write a 16-bit halfword in little-endian byte order at addr.\n\ndef read32L211–217 ↗#Read a 32-bit word in little-endian byte order at addr.\n\ndef write32L219–225 ↗#Write a 32-bit word in little-endian byte order at addr.\n\ndef read64L227–237 ↗#Read a 64-bit doubleword in little-endian byte order at addr.\n\ndef write64L239–249 ↗#Write a 64-bit doubleword in little-endian byte order at addr.\n\ndef readSizedL251–264 ↗#Read memory of size bytes (B8, H16, W32, X64), zero-extended or sign-extended to 64 bits.\n\ndef writeSizedL266–272 ↗#Write memory of size bytes (B8, H16, W32, X64) from a 64-bit value.\n\ndef read128L274–278 ↗#Read a 128-bit quadword in little-endian byte order at addr.\n\ndef write128L280–284 ↗#Write a 128-bit quadword in little-endian byte order at addr.\n\ndef readSimdSizedL286–293 ↗#Read memory of SimdMemSize (B8, H16, S32, D64, Q128), zero-extended to 128 bits.\n\ndef writeSimdSizedL295–302 ↗#Write memory of SimdMemSize (B8, H16, S32, D64, Q128) from a 128-bit SIMD register value.\n\ndef readBytesL304–308 ↗#Read count contiguous bytes starting at base ([m.read8 (base + 0), m.read8 (base + 1), ..., m.read8 (base + (count - 1))]).\n\ndef writeBytesL310–314 ↗#Write a contiguous list of bytes starting at base, so that byte i lands at base + i. Used to load statically initialized data (e.g. an exported ELF .rodata image) into memory.\n\nPermissions\n\ndef allPermL318–321 ↗#Every byte of [addr, addr + n) (wrapping modulo 2^64) has an optional permission satisfying p.\n\ndef allNoLazyFreeL323–326 ↗#Every byte of [addr, addr + n) (wrapping modulo 2^64) has isLazyFree = false.\n\ndef allAnonL328–331 ↗#Every byte of [addr, addr + n) (wrapping modulo 2^64) is mapped anonymous (isAnon = true).\n\ndef canReadL333–337 ↗#[addr, addr + n) is deterministically readable (r = true and not marked lazyFree via MADV_FREE). Reading an unwritten lazyFree byte fails closed because Linux 7.3 may asynchronously reclaim the page to zero between loads (mm/rmap.c ttu_anon_lazyfree_folio).\n\ndef canWriteL339–342 ↗#[addr, addr + n) is writable: w and not x. Stores to executable memory always fault, so the code of a loaded program never changes.\n\ndef canExecL344–346 ↗#[addr, addr + n) is executable.\n\ndef canMapL348–350 ↗#[addr, addr + n) is unmapped (!(m.perm a).mapped for every byte).\n\ndef countPermL352–356 ↗#Number of bytes in [addr, addr + n) (wrapping modulo 2^64) whose optional permission satisfies p.\n\ndef countRwL358–360 ↗#Number of bytes in [addr, addr + n) mapped with Perm.rw (m.perm a == some Perm.rw).\n\ndef FrozenL362–363 ↗#Byte a is frozen in m: mapped read-only or executable and not writable (Option.frozen).\n\ndef KeepsFrozenL365–372 ↗#m' keeps every byte that is frozen in m: same contents, same permission, same lazyFree bit, and same anonymous-mapping bit (isAnon). Every Step does this (instructions only store to canWrite bytes; extern hooks must, see Step.extern), so code and read-only data stay loaded throughout execution.\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 374–382): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Memory.mapFresh\n\ndef mapZeroL384–398 ↗#Map the currently unmapped bytes of [base, base + len) read-write and zero-filled; mapped bytes are unchanged. Used for the fresh pages of brk growth and anonymous mmap, which Linux zero-fills. Never touches frozen bytes.\n\n\n\ndef SimdMemSize.bytesL393–395 ↗#Number of bytes accessed by a SIMD load or store of size sz.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/ISA/Memory.lean","id":"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.ISA.Memory/#file-Arm64___Trusted___ISA___Memory"}});